    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d907">

                <pb facs="0969" n="1803" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1803"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/407</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Garrett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">The Hague</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-05-03T19:00:00+01:19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 3, 1918, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received May 4, 9.41 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2452. My 2426, April 29, 5 p.m. The Minister for Foreign Affairs has just told me
                    that the negotiations with Germany seem nearly to have reached an agreement in
                    the sense of the statement sent you in the above telegram, that is to say: <list>
                        <label>1. (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Germany has agreed to the limit of 1,600,000 tons per annum and will
                            give an undertaking that this will not be used for military
                            purposes.</item>
                        <label/>
                        <item>(<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) 250,000 tons per month is recognized
                            as a maximum that will probably never be reached. As to the exchange for
                            coal and iron that does not seem to enter in at the moment.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>Agreed to as to the ships at Antwerp.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>Agreed to except that there is not yet an understanding as to what
                            constitutes military “arms”.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>Agreed to.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Loudon professes to
                    believe that the final agreement will be reached.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Garrett</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
